# transition.mixer

Motion blends numbers, colors and strings that share the same shape, such as
two paths with the same commands. `transition.mixer` (Motion 14.8) lets you
blend the rest yourself. The typical case is morphing SVG paths with different
numbers of points.

```svelte
<motion.path
  animate={{ d }}
  transition={{ mixer: morph }}
/>
```

<Example isSmall exampleUrl="/examples/transition-mixer">
  <TransitionMixerExample />
</Example>

## What a mixer is

A mixer is a function that takes the value an animation starts from and the
value it goes to. It returns either a function that maps progress to a value,
or `undefined` to blend them as usual.

Progress runs from `0` to `1`, and can go beyond them with springs and
overshooting easing.

```ts
mixer?: (from: any, to: any) => ((progress: number) => any) | undefined
```

It works anywhere a transition does: `motion.*` components, `animate()`
(including sequences) and `useAnimate()`.

## Morphing SVG paths

This mixer slides a square's last corner out of a triangle's. The default mixer
can't do it, as the two paths have different numbers of points:

```svelte
<script>
  import { motion } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'

  const triangle = 'M10 10L90 10L90 90Z'
  const square = 'M10 10L90 10L90 90L10 90Z'

  const corner = () => (p) =>
    p >= 1 ? square : `M10 10L90 10L90 90L${90 - 80 * p} 90Z`
</script>

<svg viewBox="0 0 100 100">
  <motion.path
    d={triangle}
    animate={{ d: square }}
    transition={{ duration: 1, mixer: corner }}
  />
</svg>
```

For arbitrary shapes, a library like [flubber](https://github.com/veltman/flubber)
can be the mixer:

```ts
import { interpolate } from 'flubber'

const morph = (from: string, to: string) => interpolate(from, to)
```

Path data is written to the `d` attribute. Setting `d` to `undefined` removes
the attribute.

## Return `undefined` to blend as usual

A mixer can choose which values it handles. When it returns `undefined`, Motion
blends that pair the way it would without a mixer:

```ts
const mixer = (from, to) =>
  typeof from === 'string' && from.startsWith('M') ? morph(from, to) : undefined
```

## Numbers still blend normally

Animations between numbers, like `opacity` or `x`, never call the mixer. They
run exactly as they would without it.

## Where it runs

Animations whose mixer returns a function for at least one pair run on the main
thread, because the browser can't call a JavaScript mixer. The rest, including
numbers and pairs where the mixer returns `undefined`, run as they would
without it, so they can still be hardware accelerated.

Animations that only run in the browser, like those from `animateMini`
(Motion's `motion/mini`), don't use a mixer.

> **Tip**: a mixer can be asked for the same pair more than once, so keep any
> costly setup inside the function it returns, for when that function is first
> called:
>
> ```ts
> const morph = (from: string, to: string) => {
>   let mix: ReturnType<typeof interpolate> | undefined
>   return (progress: number) => {
>     mix ??= interpolate(from, to)
>     return mix(progress)
>   }
> }
> ```

## API Reference

```ts
type Transition = {
  // ...
  mixer?: (from: any, to: any) => ((progress: number) => any) | undefined
}
```

Based on Motion's `transition.mixer` (Motion 14.8).
